#!/bin/bash
# post-land-check — runs the checks `land` promises after a land, so none of them depends on anyone remembering
# (her ask 23 Nov 2026: "close both gaps"). It reads the DUE lines `land` writes into ~/Work/tasks/post-land-checks.md
# (the one list of post-land checks; they moved out of ~/HANDOFF.md on 27 Nov 2026, and nothing reads them there):
#
#   - ENG-CHECK DUE <owner/repo>#<pr> <sha> — …   a land no fresh reviewer read (her bypass, or a Fast PR on
#                                                  green CI). Fast PRs item 1: ONE batched engineering check of
#                                                  the day's merged diffs, same day, never holding a land.
#   - FORM-PROOF DUE <owner/repo>#<pr> <sha> — …  a land that touched a form (land's gate_forms). Her words
#                                                  23 Nov 2026: "after theee fixes u need to test them"; the
#                                                  live form gets a marked TEST submission right after deploy.
#   - OUTCOME-CHECK DUE <owner/repo>#<pr> <sha> — task:<slug> by:<day> expect:>=N — outcome: … — check: <cmd>
#                                                  a green land whose PR declared a real Outcome (land's Outcome
#                                                  gate, her order 24 Nov 2026: nothing is "working" until its real
#                                                  output is checked live the next day). expect:infer = her bypass.
#
#   post-land-check                 every DUE line: each FORM-PROOF on its own, then all ENG-CHECKs in ONE batch,
#                                   then each OUTCOME-CHECK whose by-day has come
#   post-land-check --now <key>     only the FORM-PROOF line for <owner/repo>#<pr> (land starts this, detached)
#   post-land-check --dry-run       print what would run, run nothing
#
# Meaning is a model's job: each check is ONE `ai-run --role brain` (a fresh Claude that never built the PR).
# Code only checks structure: after the run, the PR must carry a NEW comment from this account whose first line
# is `Engineering check: PASS|FAIL — …` or `Form proof (production): PASS|FAIL — …`. That comment, never the
# model's own word, turns the line DONE or FAIL (with the comment's link). No such comment leaves the line DUE
# and the exit non-zero (rule 0: a run that proved nothing never ends 0). A FAIL, or a run that proved nothing,
# raises a macOS banner; a form FAIL also files a GitHub issue on that repo (GitHub emails her: a broken live
# form is a break a real person hits). Never an Open Loops row (her ruling 22 Nov 2026).
# An engineering FAIL is never only a mark (her yes 24 Nov 2026: "a FAIL would then always lead to a fix"): it
# writes the fix's task sheet ~/Work/tasks/engfix-<repo>-<pr>.md (STATUS: TODO, the verdict link, how to land),
# which the coordinator picks up like any open sheet. The fix lands with `land-after <pr> --task <that slug>`,
# which moves the sheet to done/. Every run, due work or not, banners each fix sheet still open, so a FAIL
# cannot be forgotten: the banner repeats nightly until the fix has landed.
#
# An OUTCOME-CHECK is decided in three steps, and nothing skips the first two. (1) CODE runs the PR's own check
# through `outcome-read` (beside this file): no shell, none of her logins, only fixed read tools (a public GET with
# no token, a file in the task or log folders, text tools on stdin). A check that needs anything else is never run
# and is COULDNT-CHECK ("the check was not run"). 5-min timeout. Code reads the count it printed (an integer on its
# last line, else its non-empty line count). A read that did not answer (a site down, GitHub's limit, a missing
# file) or no output is COULDNT-CHECK, never a count; a printed 0 is a reading. A count under the floor is FAIL (NOT
# WORKING), posted by code with no model run. (2) Only a count that meets the floor goes to ONE fresh model with NO
# tools (`ai-run` with AI_RUN_BARE=1: no shell, no files, no connectors, none of her settings), which is given the
# output and the PR's text and answers `Outcome check (live): PASS|FAIL|COULDNT-CHECK — <what it saw, with counts>`;
# CODE posts that as the PR comment. A count below the floor never reaches it, and the model's FAIL stands. No
# verdict line, ai-run down or a usage limit is COULDNT-CHECK. A line with no Outcome declared (her bypass,
# expect:infer) first asks the same no-tools model to WRITE one check in outcome-read's tools; code runs it the same
# way. (3) PASS → the line DONE, the task sheet's line 1 DONE (moved to done/), a
# Notices line "<repo> #<pr> <outcome>: working — <seen>". FAIL → FAIL, line 1 FAIL, a banner, "… NOT WORKING —
# <seen>". COULDNT-CHECK → the line stays DUE, exit 1, a "… NOT CHECKED — <seen>" Notices line each day, and a
# banner once it is 2+ days past its by-day. One try per line per night (a replay posts nothing twice). Public
# comments and Notices carry counts only (emails masked); the check's raw output stays in the local log.
#
# OPEN LOOPS "LANDED, WAITING ON PROOF" (her words 27 Nov 2026: "we need a new section on open loops for the not yet
# proven stuff and move it off the main list"): after an outcome verdict for <repo>#<pr>, every open row in that
# section whose proof_pr is exactly that PR is closed on PASS ("<repo> #<pr> live check passed <date>: <seen>") or
# returned to the main list on FAIL (next date today, next step "NOT WORKING (<date>): <seen> — …"), through
# open-loops-proof.mjs (beside this file). COULDNT-CHECK touches no row. Open Loops down: the rows stay as they are,
# the log says NAMED STOP [OPEN_LOOPS_UNREACHABLE] and the Notices line says the row was not updated.
#
# Scheduled nightly by launchd/com.seq.post-land-check.plist (23:15 Central, inside the Mac's 22:00–07:00
# window; an ai-run job, so it stays on the Mac: her ruling 22 Nov 2026). Exit: 0 every line got a verdict
# (FAILs included: they are results, and they are raised) · 1 a line got no verdict · 2 the list is missing or unreadable.
# Env (tests): POST_LAND_DUE_FILE, POST_LAND_AI_RUN, POST_LAND_LOG, POST_LAND_NOTIFY (a command given the text),
#   POST_LAND_TASKS (task sheets dir), POST_LAND_TODAY (YYYY-MM-DD), POST_LAND_OUTCOME_READ (the reader; tests),
#   POST_LAND_NOTICE (a command given key and text; default: boss-os scripts/ops/outcome-notice.mjs via the vault),
#   POST_LAND_LOCKS, POST_LAND_LOOPS (a command given pr, verdict, seen and day; default: open-loops-proof.mjs via the vault).
set -uo pipefail

DUE_FILE="${POST_LAND_DUE_FILE:-$HOME/Work/tasks/post-land-checks.md}"
AI_RUN="${POST_LAND_AI_RUN:-$HOME/bin/ai-run}"
LOG="${POST_LAND_LOG:-$HOME/Work/tasks/logs/post-land-check.log}"
# HER PAUSE (owner, 25 Nov 2026: "cancel the next day checks tomorrow just for tomorrow b/c im out of usage").
# A run on a day before the date in the skip file does nothing but say so; every DUE
# line stays DUE and is checked on the first run after. The file expires by its own date. It covers EVERY run,
# land's immediate `--now` form proofs too (25 Nov 2026: each land under her bypass started a model-run form proof
# while she was out of usage); POST_LAND_FORCE=1 runs one anyway.
# ONE PAUSE FILE (27 Nov 2026): the same date file ai-run reads (`ai-pause <date>`), so one pause covers every
# scheduled AI job; this script's own skip-until file is gone.
SKIP_FILE="${POST_LAND_SKIP:-${AI_RUN_PAUSE_FILE:-$HOME/.boss-os/ai-pause-until}}"
if [ "${POST_LAND_FORCE:-}" != 1 ] && [ -r "$SKIP_FILE" ]; then
  skip_until="$(tr -d '[:space:]' < "$SKIP_FILE")"
  if [[ "$skip_until" =~ ^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}$ ]] && [[ "$(date +%Y-%m-%d)" < "$skip_until" ]]; then
    msg="post-land-check: PAUSED by the owner until $skip_until ($SKIP_FILE); no check ran, every DUE line stays DUE"
    echo "$msg"; mkdir -p "$(dirname "$LOG")" 2>/dev/null; echo "$(date '+%F %T') $msg" >> "$LOG" 2>/dev/null
    exit 0
  fi
fi
# THE CHECK HAS NO SHELL AND NO LOGINS (rebuilt 27 Nov 2026; her finish line: "runs every night with no shell, only
# allowed read tools, and no access to her logins"). The first design handed the check to `bash -c` with her gh and
# wrangler logins and tried to SPOT a write with a hand-built shell parser; it failed review twice. Now the check's
# text never reaches a shell: `outcome-read` (beside this file) splits it into words itself and does each read with
# a fixed tool that can only read (a public GET with no token, a file in the task/log folders, a text tool on stdin
# in a no-network, no-write sandbox). Anything else is refused, never run. `land` asks the same file
# (`outcome-read --explain`) when the PR is opened, so a check that cannot run is caught before it lands.
OUTCOME_READ="${POST_LAND_OUTCOME_READ:-$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" && pwd)/outcome-read}"
TASKS="${POST_LAND_TASKS:-$HOME/Work/tasks}"
NOW_KEY=""; DRY=""
while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
  case "$1" in
    --now) NOW_KEY="${2:-}"; shift ;;
    --dry-run) DRY=1 ;;
    -h|--help) sed -n '2,50p' "$0"; exit 0 ;;
    *) echo "post-land-check: unknown argument $1" >&2; exit 64 ;;
  esac
  shift
done
mkdir -p "$(dirname "$LOG")" 2>/dev/null
say() { printf '%s %s\n' "$(TZ=America/Chicago date '+%d %b %H:%M CT')" "$*" | tee -a "$LOG"; }
notify() {
  if [ -n "${POST_LAND_NOTIFY:-}" ]; then "$POST_LAND_NOTIFY" "$1"; return; fi
  osascript -e "display notification \"${1//\"/\'}\" with title \"Post-land check\"" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
}

[ -f "$DUE_FILE" ] && [ -r "$DUE_FILE" ] || { say "NAMED STOP [DUE_FILE_UNREADABLE] $DUE_FILE is missing or cannot be read: no DUE line could be checked"; notify "Post-land check could not read its list, $DUE_FILE"; exit 2; }

# An engineering FAIL's fix sheet. One per PR: an existing open or done sheet is never overwritten. Line 1
# never names <owner/repo>#<n>: land-after reads that as the sheet's own PR and would refuse the FIX PR's land.
fix_sheet() { # $1 owner/repo#pr  $2 verdict url
  local repo="${1%#*}" pr="${1##*#}" slug sheet
  slug="engfix-${repo##*/}-$pr"; sheet="$TASKS/$slug.md"
  { [ -e "$sheet" ] || [ -e "$TASKS/done/$slug.md" ]; } && return 0
  mkdir -p "$TASKS" 2>/dev/null
  if printf '%s\n' \
      "STATUS: TODO engineering-check FAIL on ${repo##*/} PR $pr, fix it today — $2" \
      "# Fix the engineering-check FAIL on $1" "" \
      "Written by post-land-check $(TZ=America/Chicago date '+%d %b %Y %H:%M CT'). The verdict: $2" "" \
      "1. Read the verdict comment. Fix every CONFIRMED finding (and each SUSPECTED one that proves real) in ONE PR" \
      "   on $repo, briefed with agent-brief like any fix; a refactor the verdict names is built, never logged as debt." \
      "2. Land it: land-after <fix pr> --task $slug --repo $repo. Its PASS moves this sheet to done/, which ends the" \
      "   nightly banner." > "$sheet" 2>/dev/null
  then say "  fix sheet: $sheet"; else say "  (the fix sheet $sheet could not be written; the FAIL line and banner stand)"; fi
}
# Every fix sheet still open (not yet moved to done/ by its land): the nightly reminder.
open_fixes() { local f; for f in "$TASKS"/engfix-*.md; do [ -f "$f" ] && basename "$f" .md; done; return 0; }

# The DUE lines: kind <TAB> key <TAB> sha <TAB> the rest.
due_lines() {
  sed -nE 's/^- (ENG-CHECK|FORM-PROOF) DUE ([^ ]+#[0-9]+) ([^ ]+) — (.*)$/\1\t\2\t\3\t\4/p' "$DUE_FILE"
}
# DUE -> DONE|FAIL for one kind+key, the verdict's link appended. Same lock as land and the compaction hook.
mark() { # $1 kind  $2 key  $3 DONE|FAIL  $4 link
  python3 - "$DUE_FILE" "$1" "$2" "$3" "$4" <<'PY'
import os, re, sys, fcntl
path, kind, key, verdict, link = sys.argv[1:6]
lock = open(os.path.join(os.environ.get("TMPDIR", "/tmp"), "handoff-md.lock"), "w"); fcntl.flock(lock, fcntl.LOCK_EX)
doc = open(path).read()
pat = re.compile(r"(?m)^- " + re.escape(kind) + r" DUE " + re.escape(key) + r" (.*)$")
doc2, n = pat.subn(lambda m: "- " + kind + " " + verdict + " " + key + " " + m.group(1) + " → " + link, doc, count=1)
if n:
    tmp = path + ".plc.tmp"; open(tmp, "w").write(doc2); os.replace(tmp, path)
PY
}
# The newest comment on the PR, posted after $3 by this gh account, whose first line starts with $2.
# stdout: "PASS|FAIL<TAB>url"; rc 1 none.
verdict_comment() { # $1 owner/repo#pr  $2 marker  $3 ISO start time
  local repo="${1%#*}" pr="${1##*#}" me
  me="$(gh api user --jq .login 2>/dev/null)" || return 1
  gh api --paginate "repos/$repo/issues/$pr/comments" 2>/dev/null \
    | jq -rs --arg me "$me" --arg since "$3" --arg m "$2" '
        add // [] | map(select(.user.login == $me and .created_at >= $since))
        | map(. + {first: ((.body // "") | split("\n")[0] | gsub("^[#*> ]+"; ""))})
        | map(select(.first | ascii_downcase | startswith($m | ascii_downcase)))
        | last // empty
        | ((.first | ascii_downcase | ltrimstr($m | ascii_downcase) | gsub("^[ :]+"; "") | ascii_upcase | .[0:4]) + "\t" + .html_url)' \
    | grep -E '^(PASS|FAIL)	' | tail -1 | grep . || return 1
}

ENG_PROMPT_HEAD='You are a FRESH, independent reviewer (~/.claude/agents/reviewer.md). You did not build any of these PRs.
They landed today with no reviewer reading them (her bypass, or a Fast PR on green CI). This is the ONE batched same-day
engineering check her Fast PRs ruling promises (23 Nov 2026). Load the ship-ready skill, engineering.md: automatic fails 1-6.
For EACH merged PR below: read its merged diff, the first 60,000 characters only (gh pr diff <n> --repo <owner/repo> | head -c 60000; never
print a whole diff, a whole file or a whole log: use grep and sed -n ranges for the code around it), and judge it
against fails 1-6 plus the one question "built from scratch today with our tools, would it look like this?". Also check its
"Forms:" line is honest: if it touches a form page or server path and says none, that is a FAIL.
Judge its "Outcome:" line and "## Not built" section by MEANING, against its brief and task sheet (~/Work/tasks/<slug>.md and
<slug>-brief.md, named on its OUTCOME-CHECK line or in the PR; read them when they exist), never by keywords: an Outcome that
is not the real output a person or job would see (a test run, a merged PR or "CI green" called the outcome), a check that would
count something other than that output, or "none" on a PR that changes what a job produces, is a FAIL. So is a brief item, or a
promise in the PR body, that is not built and is not listed under "## Not built" word for word.
Then post ONE comment on that PR (gh pr comment <n> --repo <owner/repo> --body-file <file>) whose FIRST line is exactly
  Engineering check: PASS — <what you read, one line>
or
  Engineering check: FAIL — <one line>
followed by each finding as CONFIRMED/SUSPECTED with file:line and the fix. A fail blocks nothing now (it landed); it is fixed
by a same-day follow-up PR the coordinator opens from your comment. Read-only: never push, merge, deploy, open a PR or send
anything except these comments. Every repo is public: no LP names, deal terms, owner emails or secrets in a comment.
THE PRs:'

form_prompt() { # $1 key  $2 sha  $3 the rest of the line
  cat <<EOF
You are a FRESH checker. PR $1 (merge $2) just landed and was deployed. Its line says: $3
Her rule (23 Nov 2026): "u need to make sure the forms are not broken … after theee fixes u need to test them". Prove every
form this PR touches works on PRODUCTION now.
1. Read the PR (gh pr view ${1##*#} --repo ${1%#*}; gh pr diff ...) and find every form whose page or server path it changed
   ("undeclared" means you decide from the diff; if it touches no form at all, say so in the verdict, PASS).
2. For each form: open its live production page, then make ONE real submission, clearly marked TEST in the name, subject and
   message (e.g. name "TEST post-land-check", message "TEST — automated check after PR ${1##*#}, ignore"). Use the repo's
   test route or test recipient when it has one (read its docs/ and the form handler). Test submissions need no yes (her
   blanket approval, 23 Nov 2026) on any host, but NEVER to a real customer, never with a real person's details, and if the
   form charges money, stop at the payment step and say so. Confirm it was RECEIVED (the success state AND the server's answer;
   the stored row or the delivered email where you can read it), then mark that row test if the system has a way to.
3. Post ONE comment on the PR (gh pr comment ${1##*#} --repo ${1%#*} --body-file <file>) whose FIRST line is exactly
     Form proof (production): PASS — <each form, the TEST submission, the proof it was received>
   or
     Form proof (production): FAIL — <what broke, one line>
   followed by the evidence (status codes, request or row ids, never a secret or a person's details).
Never change code, push, merge or deploy. Every repo is public: no owner emails or secrets in the comment.
EOF
}

run_ai() { # $1 label  $2 prompt -> rc of ai-run
  [ -n "$DRY" ] && { say "[dry-run] would run ai-run --role brain for $1"; return 0; }
  # USAGE SAVER (her approved changes, built 27 Nov 2026): a routine check runs on Sonnet, starts with no connectors
  # (it reads a diff and a command's output), and is capped at 15 minutes a check and 200 checks a day. The pause was
  # already honoured above (or overridden by POST_LAND_FORCE=1), so ai-run is told not to stop on it a second time.
  AI_RUN_CALLER=post-land-check AI_RUN_CLAUDE_MODEL="${POST_LAND_MODEL:-sonnet}" AI_RUN_MCP=none \
    AI_RUN_MAX_SECS="${POST_LAND_MAX_SECS:-900}" AI_RUN_MAX_RUNS="${POST_LAND_MAX_RUNS:-200}" AI_RUN_IGNORE_PAUSE=1 \
    "$AI_RUN" --role brain --network --cwd "$HOME" --out "$LOG" -- "$2" </dev/null
}
# One run per line at a time: land's detached --now and the nightly run never both submit the same form.
LOCKS="${POST_LAND_LOCKS:-$HOME/.cache/post-land-check}"; mkdir -p "$LOCKS" 2>/dev/null
HELD=()
take() { # $1 key -> rc 1 when another run holds it
  local d="$LOCKS/$(printf '%s' "$1" | tr -c 'A-Za-z0-9._-' '_').lock"
  if mkdir "$d" 2>/dev/null; then HELD+=("$d"); return 0; fi
  # A lock older than 2 hours belongs to a run that died: take it over.
  if [ -n "$(find "$d" -maxdepth 0 -mmin +120 2>/dev/null)" ]; then HELD+=("$d"); return 0; fi
  return 1
}
# On the way out of a nightly run (every exit below, "nothing due" included), after the verdicts: lessons-pass drafts
# check lines from the day's failures. Its exit never changes this script's (an EXIT trap keeps the exit code).
trap 'for d in "${HELD[@]:-}"; do [ -n "$d" ] && rmdir "$d" 2>/dev/null; done; [ -n "$NOW_KEY$DRY" ] || "${POST_LAND_LESSONS:-$HOME/bin/lessons-pass}" >> "$LOG" 2>&1 || { say "NAMED STOP [LESSONS_NOT_WRITTEN] lessons-pass did not finish; see ~/Work/tasks/lessons"; notify "Lessons pass did not finish: see ~/Work/tasks/lessons"; }' EXIT

# The fixes earlier FAILs still owe, before any new check (and on nights with nothing due).
if [ -z "$NOW_KEY" ] && [ -z "$DRY" ]; then
  owed="$(open_fixes | tr '\n' ' ' | sed 's/ $//')"
  if [ -n "$owed" ]; then
    say "engineering-check fixes still open: $owed"
    notify "Engineering-check fixes still open (each is a sheet in ~/Work/tasks): $owed"
  fi
fi

fails=0; unproved=0; ran=0
LINES="$(due_lines)"
[ -n "$NOW_KEY" ] && LINES="$(awk -F'\t' -v k="$NOW_KEY" '$1 == "FORM-PROOF" && $2 == k' <<<"$LINES")"
if [ -z "$LINES" ] && { [ -n "$NOW_KEY" ] || ! grep -q '^- OUTCOME-CHECK DUE ' "$DUE_FILE"; }; then say "nothing due${NOW_KEY:+ for $NOW_KEY} in $DUE_FILE"; exit 0; fi

# 1. Each form on its own, first: a broken live form is the urgent one.
while IFS=$'\t' read -r kind key sha rest; do
  [ "$kind" = FORM-PROOF ] || continue
  take "$key" || { say "form proof: $key is being checked by another run"; continue; }
  ran=$((ran + 1)); start="$(date -u '+%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ')"
  say "form proof: $key ($rest)"
  run_ai "$key" "$(form_prompt "$key" "$sha" "$rest")"
  [ -n "$DRY" ] && continue
  if v="$(verdict_comment "$key" "Form proof (production)" "$start")"; then
    verdict="${v%%	*}"; url="${v#*	}"
    if [ "$verdict" = PASS ]; then mark FORM-PROOF "$key" DONE "$url"; say "  PASS $url"
    else
      mark FORM-PROOF "$key" FAIL "$url"; fails=$((fails + 1)); say "  FAIL $url"
      notify "A live form FAILED its test after ${key}: $url"
      gh issue create --repo "${key%#*}" --title "Live form check failed after #${key##*#}" \
        --body "The production form check that runs after every form change failed: $url

A real person filling this form may hit it now. Fix it today (her rule, 23 Nov 2026: forms are never left broken by a fix)." </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1 \
        || say "  (the GitHub issue could not be filed; the banner and the FAIL line stand)"
    fi
  else
    unproved=$((unproved + 1)); say "  NO VERDICT: no 'Form proof (production):' comment on $key after the run; the line stays DUE"
    notify "Form proof for $key got no verdict; the line stays DUE in post-land-checks.md"
  fi
done <<<"$LINES"

# 2. Every engineering check in ONE batch (Fast PRs item 1).
ENG="$(awk -F'\t' '$1 == "ENG-CHECK"' <<<"$LINES")"
if [ -n "$ENG" ] && [ -z "$NOW_KEY" ] && take "eng-check-batch"; then
  ran=$((ran + 1)); start="$(date -u '+%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ')"
  list="$(awk -F'\t' '{ print "- " $2 " (merge " $3 "): " $4 }' <<<"$ENG")"
  say "engineering check: $(wc -l <<<"$ENG" | tr -d ' ') PR(s) in one batch"
  run_ai "the engineering batch" "$ENG_PROMPT_HEAD
$list"
  if [ -z "$DRY" ]; then
    while IFS=$'\t' read -r kind key sha rest; do
      if v="$(verdict_comment "$key" "Engineering check" "$start")"; then
        verdict="${v%%	*}"; url="${v#*	}"
        if [ "$verdict" = PASS ]; then mark ENG-CHECK "$key" DONE "$url"; say "  PASS $key $url"
        else mark ENG-CHECK "$key" FAIL "$url"; fails=$((fails + 1)); say "  FAIL $key $url"; fix_sheet "$key" "$url"; fi
      else
        unproved=$((unproved + 1)); say "  NO VERDICT: no 'Engineering check:' comment on $key after the run; the line stays DUE"
      fi
    done <<<"$ENG"
  fi
fi

# 3. OUTCOME-CHECK lines (her order 24 Nov 2026: "nothing is called working until its real output is seen live").
#    land writes: - OUTCOME-CHECK DUE <key> <sha> — task:<slug> by:<YYYY-MM-DD> expect:>=<N>|infer — outcome: <o> — check: <cmd>
#    On or after its by-day (Central): CODE runs it through outcome-read (no shell, no logins; 5 min) or it is never
#    run (COULDNT-CHECK), counts its output, and alone decides COULDNT-CHECK (a read did not answer, or it printed
#    nothing) and FAIL (count below the floor); a PASS is then impossible. Only a count at or over the floor goes to
#    ONE fresh model with no tools, which judges whether that output really is the outcome; code posts its
#    `Outcome check (live): PASS|FAIL|COULDNT-CHECK — <what it saw, with counts>`. Its FAIL stands; its PASS counts
#    only with the floor met. No verdict line, a model outage or a usage limit is COULDNT-CHECK: the line stays DUE,
#    the Notices line says NOT CHECKED, the exit is 1.
TODAY="${POST_LAND_TODAY:-$(TZ=America/Chicago date +%F)}"
outcome_lines() { # key <TAB> sha <TAB> task <TAB> by <TAB> expect <TAB> outcome <TAB> check
  sed -nE 's/^- OUTCOME-CHECK DUE ([^ ]+#[0-9]+) ([^ ]+) — task:([^ ]*) by:([0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}) expect:(>=[0-9]+|infer) — outcome: (.*) — check: (.*)$/\1\t\2\t\3\t\4\t\5\t\6\t\7/p' "$DUE_FILE" \
    | sed -E 's/[[:space:]]+$//'
}
expected_words() { # $1 ">=N" | infer -> the expected part of a notice
  case "$1" in ">="*) printf 'at least %s' "${1#>=}" ;; *) printf 'the outcome the PR promised' ;; esac
}
days_since() { python3 -c 'import sys,datetime as d; print((d.date.fromisoformat(sys.argv[2])-d.date.fromisoformat(sys.argv[1])).days)' "$1" "$2"; }
mask() { sed -E 's/[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.-]+\.[A-Za-z]{2,}/[email]/g'; }   # public comments: counts, never an address
one_line() { tr '\n\t' '  ' | sed -E 's/\. +/; /g; s/[[:space:]]+/ /g; s/^ //; s/ $//' | cut -c1-220; }
run_check() { # $1 check -> stdout its output; rc 0 it ran · 3 refused, never run · 4 could not read · 124 timed out
  [ -x "$OUTCOME_READ" ] || { echo "could not read: outcome-read is missing at $OUTCOME_READ, so no check is run"; return 4; }
  timeout 300 "$OUTCOME_READ" "$1" </dev/null 2>&1
}
count_of() { # stdin output -> the bare integer on its last non-empty line, else its number of non-empty lines
  awk 'NF { n++; last=$0 } END { gsub(/^[ \t]+|[ \t]+$/, "", last); if (last ~ /^[0-9]+$/) print last+0; else print n+0 }'
}
ask_model() { # $1 prompt -> stdout the model's whole answer; rc of ai-run
  # A model with NO tools (AI_RUN_BARE=1): it reads its prompt and answers in text. No shell, no files, no
  # connectors, none of her settings or logins. Everything it needs is in the prompt; code does every read and post.
  local o rc; o="$(mktemp)"
  AI_RUN_CALLER=post-land-check AI_RUN_CLAUDE_MODEL="${POST_LAND_MODEL:-sonnet}" AI_RUN_BARE=1 \
    AI_RUN_MAX_SECS="${POST_LAND_MAX_SECS:-900}" AI_RUN_MAX_RUNS="${POST_LAND_MAX_RUNS:-200}" AI_RUN_IGNORE_PAUSE=1 \
    "$AI_RUN" --role brain --cwd "$LOCKS" --out "$o" -- "$1" </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1; rc=$?
  cat "$o" >> "$LOG" 2>/dev/null; cat "$o"; rm -f "$o"; return "$rc"
}
answer_line() { # $1 label (e.g. "Check")  stdin the model's answer -> the text after the first "<label>:" line; rc 1 none
  sed -nE "s/^[[:space:]#*>\`-]*$1:[[:space:]]*//p" | sed -E 's/[[:space:]*`]+$//; s/^`+//' | grep -m1 .
}
verdict_of() { # stdin the model's answer -> "PASS|FAIL|COULDNT<TAB>what it saw"; rc 1 no verdict line
  sed -nE 's/^[[:space:]#*>-]*[Oo]utcome check \(live\):[[:space:]*]*//p' | head -1 | awk '
    { u = toupper($0)
      if (u ~ /^PASS/) v = "PASS"; else if (u ~ /^FAIL/) v = "FAIL"; else if (u ~ /^COULDN.?T/) v = "COULDNT"; else exit 1
      sub(/^[A-Za-z\047’-]+[ *]*(—|–|-)*[ ]*/, ""); gsub(/\t/, " "); if ($0 == "") exit 1
      print v "\t" $0; found = 1 }
    END { exit found ? 0 : 1 }'
}
pr_text() { # $1 key  $2 "diff" to add the changed files and the diff's first 20,000 characters -> the PR as text; rc 1 unread
  local repo="${1%#*}" pr="${1##*#}" body
  body="$(gh pr view "$pr" --repo "$repo" --json title,body --jq '.title + "\n\n" + .body' </dev/null 2>/dev/null | head -c 6000)" || return 1
  [ -n "$body" ] || return 1
  printf '%s\n' "$body"
  [ "${2:-}" = diff ] || return 0
  printf '\n--- the diff (first 20,000 characters) ---\n'; gh pr diff "$pr" --repo "$repo" </dev/null 2>/dev/null | head -c 20000
}
post_comment() { # $1 key  $2 body -> stdout the comment's url (rc 1 not posted)
  local f; f="$(mktemp)"; printf '%s\n' "$2" > "$f"
  gh pr comment "${1##*#}" --repo "${1%#*}" --body-file "$f" </dev/null 2>/dev/null | grep -oE 'https://[^ ]+' | tail -1 | grep .; local rc=$?
  rm -f "$f"; return "$rc"
}
outcome_mark() { # $1 key  $2 DONE|FAIL  $3 link: the OUTCOME-CHECK line, same lock as land and the compaction hook
  mark OUTCOME-CHECK "$1" "$2" "$3"
}
sheet_set() { # $1 slug  $2 key  $3 status line  $4 to-done(1|"") — only a sheet whose line 1 names this PR
  local f="$TASKS/$1.md" tmp
  [ -n "$1" ] && [ "$1" != - ] && [ -f "$f" ] || { say "  (task sheet $1 not open: line 1 not changed)"; return 0; }
  head -1 "$f" | grep -qF " $2 " || { say "  (task sheet $1 names another PR on line 1: not changed)"; return 0; }
  tmp="$(mktemp "$TASKS/.$1.XXXXXX")" && { printf '%s\n' "$3"; tail -n +2 "$f"; } > "$tmp" && mv -f "$tmp" "$f"
  if [ -n "${4:-}" ]; then mkdir -p "$TASKS/done"; local d="$TASKS/done/$1.md"; [ -e "$d" ] && d="$TASKS/done/$1.$(date '+%Y%m%d-%H%M%S').md"; mv "$f" "$d"; fi
}
# The Notices line on her next morning email (boss-os morning "notices" section). One write per key: a key already
# written (marker in $LOCKS) is never written twice; NOT CHECKED carries the day, so it shows again each morning.
NOTICE_CMD="${POST_LAND_NOTICE:-}"
notice() { # $1 key  $2 text -> rc 0 written (or already written)
  local m="$LOCKS/notice-$(printf '%s' "$1" | tr -c 'A-Za-z0-9._-' '_')"
  [ -e "$m" ] && { say "  (Notices line $1 already written)"; return 0; }
  [ -n "$DRY" ] && { say "[dry-run] would write Notices: $2"; return 0; }
  if [ -n "$NOTICE_CMD" ]; then "$NOTICE_CMD" "$1" "$2" </dev/null
  else (cd "$HOME/.boss-os/runtime" && npm run --silent vault:run -- node scripts/ops/outcome-notice.mjs --key "$1" --text "$2" </dev/null)
  fi >>"$LOG" 2>&1 || { say "  NAMED STOP [NOTICE_UNWRITTEN] the Notices line for $1 could not be written (see $LOG); the line in the list and the PR comment stand"; return 1; }
  printf '%s Notices: %s\n' "$(TZ=America/Chicago date '+%d %b %H:%M CT')" "$2" >> "$LOG"   # the line she reads, kept in the local log
  : > "$m"
}
LOOPS_CMD="${POST_LAND_LOOPS:-}"
PROOF_JS="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" && pwd)/open-loops-proof.mjs"
proof_rows() { # $1 key  $2 PASS|FAIL  $3 seen -> rc 0 rows updated (or none waiting) | 1 Open Loops not reached: rows unchanged
  [ -n "$DRY" ] && { say "[dry-run] would close or return the Open Loops rows waiting on $1 ($2)"; return 0; }
  local out rc
  if [ -n "$LOOPS_CMD" ]; then out="$("$LOOPS_CMD" "$1" "$2" "$3" "$TODAY" </dev/null 2>&1)"; rc=$?
  else out="$(cd "$HOME/.boss-os/runtime" && npm run --silent vault:run -- node "$PROOF_JS" --pr "$1" --verdict "$2" --seen "$3" --today "$TODAY" </dev/null 2>&1)"; rc=$?
  fi
  printf '%s\n' "$out" | sed 's/^/  open-loops: /' >> "$LOG"
  if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then
    say "  NAMED STOP [OPEN_LOOPS_UNREACHABLE] the Open Loops rows waiting on $1 were not updated and stay as they are (see $LOG)"; return 1
  fi
  printf '%s\n' "$out" | grep -E '^OPEN_LOOPS (CLOSED|RETURNED) ' | sed "s/^/  open-loops: /" | while IFS= read -r l; do say "$l"; done
  return 0
}
READ_TOOLS='A check is ONE read and its pipes. No shell runs it, so no variables, no $(…), no ; && ||, no redirects.
  first, one of:  gh api <path> [--jq <filter>]      a GET of the public GitHub API, with no sign-in (every repo is public)
                  curl -fsS[L] [-A <agent>] <https url>      a GET of a public page
                  curl -s -o /dev/null -w '"'"'%{http_code}'"'"' <https url>      a public page'"'"'s status code
                  cat|grep|wc|head|tail <file>, ls <folder>      only under ~/Work/tasks, ~/Library/Logs, ~/bin, ~/HANDOFF.md
  then, after each |:  grep (-c -E -F -i -v -o -w -x), jq (-r -c -s), wc, head, tail, sort, uniq, cut, tr
Nothing behind a sign-in can be read: no D1 or database query, no admin page, no private API, no script.'
infer_prompt() { # $1 key $2 sha $3 the PR as text (with diff) $4 task sheet text
  cat <<EOF
PR $1 (merge $2) landed under the owner's bypass with no Outcome declared. Her order (24 Nov 2026): nothing is called
working until its real output is seen live. You have no tools: answer in text only. Read the PR below and decide what
real output it should have produced on the live system by now (a page, a post, a file in a repo, a scheduled run, a
log line), then write ONE check that counts that output. Code will run your check; you will not.

$READ_TOOLS

Answer with exactly these three lines and nothing else:
Outcome: <the real output, one sentence>
Check: <the check>
Expect: >= <the smallest count that means it is really happening, at least 1>
If no check built from those tools can see this PR's output, answer instead with the one line:
Check: none — <why, in plain words, one sentence>
Never count a test run, a merged PR or green CI as the outcome. Every repo is public: no names, emails or secrets.

--- the PR ---
$3
--- its task sheet ---
${4:-(none)}
EOF
}
outcome_prompt() { # $1 key $2 sha $3 outcome $4 check $5 expect $6 count $7 output(path) $8 the PR as text $9 task sheet text
  cat <<EOF
You are a FRESH, independent checker. You did not build PR $1 (merge $2). You have no tools: answer in text only.
Her order (24 Nov 2026): nothing is called working until its real output is seen live. The PR promised:
  Outcome: $3
  check: $4   (must count >= ${5#>=})
Code ran that check just now, with no shell and no sign-in. It counted $6. Its output (the first 80 lines):
----
$(head -80 "$7" | cut -c1-600)
----
Judge the MEANING: does this output show the promised outcome really happening (not a header row, an error message, a
test fixture or the old behaviour counted as the new)? If the output cannot tell you, say COULDNT-CHECK; never PASS on
a guess. Your FIRST line must be exactly
  Outcome check (live): PASS|FAIL|COULDNT-CHECK — <what the output shows, with counts, one sentence>
then up to five lines of evidence. Every repo is public: counts only, never names, LP names, deal terms, children's
names, owner emails or secrets.

--- the PR ---
${8:-(its text could not be read)}
--- its task sheet ---
${9:-(none)}
EOF
}

oruns=0; ofails=0; ounproved=0
OUTL=""; [ -z "$NOW_KEY" ] && OUTL="$(outcome_lines)"
while IFS=$'\t' read -r key sha task by expect outc cmd; do
  [ -n "$key" ] || continue
  late="$(days_since "$by" "$TODAY" 2>/dev/null)" || { say "outcome: $key has an unreadable by-day '$by'; NOT CHECKED"; late=99; }
  [ "$late" -ge 0 ] || { say "outcome: $key is due $by, not yet"; continue; }
  slugp="$(printf '%s' "$key" | sed -E 's#^[^/]+/##; s/#/ #/')"   # "boss-os #557"
  shortout="$(printf '%s' "$outc" | one_line | cut -c1-90)"
  take "outcome-$key" || { say "outcome: $key is being checked by another run"; continue; }
  tried="$LOCKS/tried-$(printf '%s' "$key" | tr -c 'A-Za-z0-9._-' '_')-$TODAY"
  oruns=$((oruns + 1))
  if [ -e "$tried" ]; then   # replay: tonight's run already said NOT CHECKED, with its comment and Notices line
    ounproved=$((ounproved + 1)); say "outcome: $key already tried today and still NOT CHECKED; no second comment"; continue
  fi
  say "outcome: $key ($outc; check: $cmd; expect $expect; by $by)"
  verdict=""; seen=""; url=""; outf="$(mktemp)"; ranline=""
  sheet_text=""; [ -n "$task" ] && [ -r "$TASKS/$task.md" ] && sheet_text="$(head -40 "$TASKS/$task.md")"
  if [ "$expect" = infer ]; then   # her bypass: no Outcome was declared, so a model with no tools writes the check; code runs it
    if [ -n "$DRY" ]; then say "[dry-run] would ask the model to write a check for $key"; rm -f "$outf"; continue; fi
    if ! prt="$(pr_text "$key" diff)"; then verdict=COULDNT; seen="the PR could not be read from GitHub"
    else
      ians="$(ask_model "$(infer_prompt "$key" "$sha" "$prt" "$sheet_text")")"
      icmd="$(printf '%s\n' "$ians" | answer_line Check)" || icmd=""
      ifloor="$(printf '%s\n' "$ians" | answer_line Expect | grep -oE '[0-9]+' | head -1)"
      if [ -z "$icmd" ]; then verdict=COULDNT; seen="the checker gave no check (it was down, out of its usage limit, or answered nothing)"
      elif printf '%s' "$icmd" | grep -qiE '^none\b'; then
        verdict=COULDNT; seen="no check can see its output without a sign-in: $(printf '%s' "$icmd" | sed -E 's/^[Nn]one[[:space:]]*(—|–|-)*[[:space:]]*//')"
      elif [ -z "$ifloor" ] || [ "$ifloor" -lt 1 ]; then verdict=COULDNT; seen="the checker gave no count to expect, so nothing it saw is proven"
      else
        cmd="$icmd"; expect=">=$ifloor"; ranline="Ran: \`$(printf '%s' "$cmd" | mask)\`"
        io="$(printf '%s\n' "$ians" | answer_line Outcome)" && outc="$io" && shortout="$(printf '%s' "$outc" | one_line | cut -c1-90)"
      fi
    fi
    if [ -n "$verdict" ]; then
      url="$(post_comment "$key" "Outcome check (live): COULDNT-CHECK — $(printf '%s' "$seen" | mask | one_line)
No Outcome was declared (owner bypass); post-land-check on $TODAY asked a model with no tools to write a check. Decided by code.")" || url="(comment not posted)"
    fi
  fi
  if [ -z "$verdict" ]; then
    run_check "$cmd" > "$outf"; crc=$?
    printf '=== outcome check %s: %s (exit %s)\n' "$key" "$cmd" "$crc" >> "$LOG"; head -200 "$outf" >> "$LOG"   # raw output: the local log only
    n="$(count_of < "$outf")"; lines_out="$(grep -c . "$outf")"
    if [ -n "$DRY" ]; then
      if [ "$crc" -eq 3 ]; then say "[dry-run] the check would not be run: $(head -1 "$outf")"; else say "[dry-run] the check printed $n (exit $crc)"; fi
      rm -f "$outf"; continue
    fi
    why=""
    if [ "$crc" -eq 3 ]; then   # refused: outside the allowed read tools. Nothing was read. Code's own verdict.
      verdict=COULDNT; seen="the check was not run, because it needs something outside the allowed read tools"
      why="Refused by outcome-read: $(head -1 "$outf" | mask)"
    elif [ "$crc" -ne 0 ] || [ "$lines_out" -eq 0 ]; then   # could not read, or silent: unknown, never a count. A printed 0 is a reading (below).
      verdict=COULDNT; seen="the check could not read the live system"   # plain words: her Notices line carries no exit codes (N6)
      [ "$crc" -eq 124 ] && seen="the check took longer than 5 minutes and was stopped"
      [ "$crc" -eq 0 ] && seen="the check printed nothing"
      [ "$crc" -eq 4 ] && why="outcome-read: $(head -1 "$outf" | mask | cut -c1-200)"
    elif [ "$n" -lt "${expect#>=}" ]; then
      verdict=FAIL; seen="saw $n, needed at least ${expect#>=}"
    fi
    if [ -n "$verdict" ]; then   # code's own verdict: no model run, no PASS possible
      url="$(post_comment "$key" "Outcome check (live): $([ "$verdict" = COULDNT ] && echo COULDNT-CHECK || echo FAIL) — $seen
${why:+$why
}Outcome: $(printf '%s' "$outc" | mask)
Check: \`$(printf '%s' "$cmd" | mask)\` (expect ${expect}), on $TODAY; no shell, no sign-in; decided by code, no model.")" || url="(comment not posted)"
    fi
  fi
  if [ -z "$verdict" ]; then   # the floor is met: ONE model with no tools judges what the output means; code posts its verdict
    ans="$(ask_model "$(outcome_prompt "$key" "$sha" "$outc" "$cmd" "$expect" "$n" "$outf" "$(pr_text "$key")" "$sheet_text")")"; arc=$?
    if v="$(printf '%s\n' "$ans" | verdict_of)"; then
      IFS=$'\t' read -r verdict seen <<<"$v"
      seen="$(printf '%s' "$seen" | mask | one_line)"
      url="$(post_comment "$key" "Outcome check (live): $([ "$verdict" = COULDNT ] && echo COULDNT-CHECK || echo "$verdict") — $seen
${ranline:+$ranline
}Outcome: $(printf '%s' "$outc" | mask)
Check: \`$(printf '%s' "$cmd" | mask)\` (expect ${expect}), run by post-land-check on $TODAY with no shell and no sign-in; it counted $n.
Judged by a model with no tools, from that output alone.")" || { url="(comment not posted)"; [ "$verdict" = COULDNT ] || { verdict=COULDNT; seen="the verdict could not be posted to the PR"; }; }
    else
      printf '=== outcome check %s: no verdict line (ai-run exit %s)\n' "$key" "$arc" >> "$LOG"
      verdict=COULDNT; seen="the checker gave no verdict (it was down, out of its usage limit, or answered nothing)"; url="(no comment)"
    fi
  fi
  rm -f "$outf"
  seen="$(printf '%s' "$seen" | mask | one_line)"
  case "$verdict" in
    PASS)
      outcome_mark "$key" DONE "$url"; say "  PASS $key $url — $seen"
      sheet_set "$task" "$key" "STATUS: DONE $key $sha $TODAY — outcome checked live: $seen ($url)" 1
      loopnote=""; proof_rows "$key" PASS "$seen" || loopnote=" (Open Loops could not be reached: a row waiting on this PR was left as it was)"
      notice "outcome:$key:working" "$slugp $shortout: working — $seen$loopnote" || ounproved=$((ounproved + 1)) ;;
    FAIL)
      outcome_mark "$key" FAIL "$url"; ofails=$((ofails + 1)); say "  FAIL $key $url — $seen"
      sheet_set "$task" "$key" "STATUS: FAIL $key $sha $TODAY — outcome NOT WORKING: $seen ($url)"
      # Three parts (her yes 25 Nov 2026): what was expected, what was seen, where to look.
      notify "NOT WORKING: $slugp $shortout — expected $(expected_words "$expect"); saw ${seen#saw }; look: $url"
      loopnote=""; proof_rows "$key" FAIL "$seen" || loopnote=" (Open Loops could not be reached: a row waiting on this PR was left as it was)"
      notice "outcome:$key:not-working" "$slugp $shortout: NOT WORKING — expected $(expected_words "$expect"); saw ${seen#saw }; look: $url$loopnote" || ounproved=$((ounproved + 1)) ;;
    *)
      ounproved=$((ounproved + 1)); : > "$tried"
      say "  COULDNT-CHECK $key — $seen; the line stays DUE"
      # NOT CHECKED is never a failed test: the check never gave a reading. It still says where to look.
      [ "$late" -ge 2 ] && notify "NOT CHECKED for $late days: $slugp $shortout — the check never gave a reading: $seen; look: $url"
      notice "outcome:$key:not-checked:$TODAY" "$slugp $shortout: NOT CHECKED — the check never gave a reading: $seen; look: $url" || true ;;
  esac
done <<<"$OUTL"
ran=$((ran + oruns)); fails=$((fails + ofails)); unproved=$((unproved + ounproved))

[ "$ran" -gt 0 ] || { say "nothing due${NOW_KEY:+ for $NOW_KEY}"; exit 0; }
[ "$fails" -eq 0 ] || notify "Post-land check: $fails FAIL(s); each is marked FAIL in ~/Work/tasks/post-land-checks.md and has a fix sheet in ~/Work/tasks"
if [ "$unproved" -gt 0 ]; then notify "Post-land check: $unproved line(s) got no verdict and stay DUE"; exit 1; fi
exit 0
